<node id="MRI Sagittal Lower Limb Mass Lesion (image)">
  <data key="d0">MRI Sagittal Lower Limb Mass Lesion (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This MRI sagittal image of the lower limb highlights a heterogeneously low-signal intensity mass lesion laterally to the semi-membranous tendon and anterior to the gastrocnemius muscle. The lesion's distribution and signal characteristics suggest the need for further clinical evaluation for a differential diagnosis, possibly related to inflammation or infection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0c49e42d66fcac95cdbce479d9fe4692</data>
  <data key="d4">PMC_PMC4531616_01.json</data>
  <data key="d5">1790435118</data>
</node>
<node id="Lower Leg MRI (Sagittal View) (medical image)">
  <data key="d0">Lower Leg MRI (Sagittal View) (medical image)</data>
  <data key="d1">medical image</data>
  <data key="d2">The image is a sagittal MRI slice of the lower leg displaying a poorly defined mass lesion laterally to the semi-membranous tendon and anterior to the gastrocnemius muscle. It suggests a potential soft tissue pathology in the context of a patient with mixed connective tissue disease, possibly leading to a differential diagnosis that includes abscess or malignancy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7092528bd06b1104facbd2062da6c532</data>
  <data key="d4">PMC_PMC4531616_01.json</data>
  <data key="d5">1790435118</data>
</node>
<node id="Knee MRI showing intra-articular mass and peripheral enhancement (image)">
  <data key="d0">Knee MRI showing intra-articular mass and peripheral enhancement (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This axial MRI slice clearly highlights a poorly defined mass lateral to the semi-membranous tendon and anterior to the gastrocnemius muscle, with surrounding hypo-intense structures consistent with inflammation. The central enhancement of the soft tissues and the hypointenese mass itself could indicate a pathological process like infection or neoplasm. The case is situated within the context of a clinical follow-up for Mycobacterium avium arthritis, considering the patient's previous history of connective tissue disease and current presentation with right knee and calf pain, along with intra-articular abscess.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-befd8f367c5b300fe816ba1568be8b49</data>
  <data key="d4">PMC_PMC4531616_01.json</data>
  <data key="d5">1790435118</data>
</node>
<node id="histopathological granulomatous inflammation sample indicative of mycobacterial infection (M. avium) (image)">
  <data key="d0">histopathological granulomatous inflammation sample indicative of mycobacterial infection (M. avium) (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">The image depicts a histopathological section showing granulomata with epithelioid histiocytes and inflammatory cells, along with acid-fast bacilli stains indicative of mycobacterial infection. This visual evidence supports the clinical diagnosis of Mycobacterium avium arthritis with extra-articular abscess in a patient with mixed connective tissue disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-84f7a1b41f501c2663cab6143153d78d</data>
  <data key="d4">PMC_PMC4531616_01.json</data>
  <data key="d5">1790435118</data>
</node>
