<node id="OGC">
  <data key="d0">OGC</data>
  <data key="d1">symptom</data>
  <data key="d2">OGC stands for oculogyric crisis, a severe movement disorder.&lt;SEP&gt;Oculogyric crisis is a symptom experienced by the patient after consuming rifampicin.&lt;SEP&gt;An oculogyric crisis (OGC) is diagnosed in the patient.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-165836f88e88091f092ccedc334c4381&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa8224d75d6912e376e2f1c68d10a040&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d87326356ec28ada0f9438ddcbd885d</data>
  <data key="d4">PMC_PMC5648305_01.json</data>
  <data key="d5">1790442280</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="trauma">
  <data key="d0">trauma</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-165836f88e88091f092ccedc334c4381</data>
  <data key="d2">Trauma was considered an alternative possible cause of OGC, although not confirmed in this case.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">PMC_PMC5648305_01.json</data>
  <data key="d5">1790441224</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Focal Nodular Opacities (Left Upper Zone) (image)">
  <data key="d0">Focal Nodular Opacities (Left Upper Zone) (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This poster-anterior (PA) chest radiograph shows focal nodular opacities in the left upper zone periphery, which are signs of granulomatous inflammation, possibly related to a drug reaction. The image is crucial for diagnosing oculogyric crisis (OGC) in a clinical case report of a 66-year-old male patient treated for tuberculosis. This finding underscores the necessity of a thorough history in identifying adverse reactions to therapy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fa8224d75d6912e376e2f1c68d10a040</data>
  <data key="d4">PMC_PMC5648305_01.json</data>
  <data key="d5">1790441494</data>
</node>
<node id="Chest X-ray of 66-year-old male TB patient with nodular opacities (image)">
  <data key="d0">Chest X-ray of 66-year-old male TB patient with nodular opacities (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">This chest X-ray provides detailed visualization of nodular opacities in the left upper zone periphery, indicative of pulmonary granulomata, observed in a clinical case of tuberculosis. The image is correlated with the clinical presentation and anti-TB medication history, contributing to the diagnosis of the oculogyric crisis and the patient's overall condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9d87326356ec28ada0f9438ddcbd885d</data>
  <data key="d4">PMC_PMC5648305_01.json</data>
  <data key="d5">1790441494</data>
</node>
<node id="ECG Showing Normal Sinus Rhythm (image)">
  <data key="d0">ECG Showing Normal Sinus Rhythm (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">The image displays an electrocardiogram (ECG) indicating normal sinus rhythm, with no arrhythmias or major deviations in QRS, T wave, or P wave patterns. This image is crucial for evaluating cardiac function and is part of a case report detailing a suspected idiosyncratic reaction to tuberculosis medication in a 66-year-old male patient.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dd181ec075dc20d751360903e9a26f3</data>
  <data key="d4">PMC_PMC5648305_01.json</data>
  <data key="d5">1790441494</data>
</node>
